---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import Panel from "~/components/illustration-kit/Panel.astro"
import Callout from "~/components/illustration-kit/Callout.astro"

const cases = [
  { car: 1, opened: 3, switchTo: 2, stayWins: true },
  { car: 2, opened: 3, switchTo: 2, stayWins: false },
  { car: 3, opened: 2, switchTo: 3, stayWins: false },
]
const doors = [1, 2, 3]
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="monty-hall-problem"
  lead="You pick door 1. Now try hiding the car behind each door."
>
  <p class="text-base leading-normal">
    The host knows where the car is and always opens another door with a goat. You can either
    stay with door 1 or switch to the only other closed door.
  </p>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    {cases.map(({ car, opened, switchTo, stayWins }, i) => (
      <Panel title={`Case ${i + 1}: the car is behind door ${car}`} class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2">
          {doors.map((door) => (
            <div
              class:list={[
                "flex min-w-0 flex-col items-center gap-1 border border-ink p-2 text-center",
                door === 1 && "bg-series-blue/20",
                door === opened && "bg-series-gold/20",
              ]}
            >
              <strong class="text-base">Door {door}</strong>
              <span class="text-base font-bold">{door === car ? "Car" : "Goat"}</span>
              <span class="text-xs leading-normal">
                {door === 1 ? "Your pick" : door === opened ? "Host opens" : "Switch to"}
              </span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-base leading-normal">
          Host opens door {opened} (goat). Switching takes you to door {switchTo}.
          <strong>
            {" "}
            {stayWins ? "Staying wins; switching loses." : "Switching wins; staying loses."}
          </strong>
        </p>
      </Panel>
    ))}
  </div>

  <Callout class="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <p class="font-bold">Stay wins 1 out of 3. Switch wins 2 out of 3.</p>
    <p class="text-base leading-normal">
      Switching wins every time your first choice was wrong. That happens in two of the three
      equally likely cases.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Fragment slot="caption">
    In the first case the host could open either goat door; door 3 is one example. The result
    assumes the host knows where the car is, always reveals a goat, and always offers a switch.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
